---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Notice from '../../components/Notice.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
import Tag from '../../components/Tag.astro';
import { product } from '../../data/site';
import report from '../../../samples/output/field-notes.default.txt?raw';
import discovery from '../../../samples/output/harbor-workspace.discovery.txt?raw';

const toc = [
  { id: 'run', label: 'Run it on a repository' },
  { id: 'what-happens', label: 'What Flowprint does first' },
  { id: 'choose-app', label: 'Choosing an app with --app' },
  { id: 'first-report', label: 'Your first report' },
  { id: 'reading-order', label: 'A reading order' },
  { id: 'full', label: 'Going further with --full' },
  { id: 'saving', label: 'Keeping a copy' },
];

const caption = `Real output of Flowprint ${product.version} (source commit ${product.sampleCommit}) on a synthetic fixture written for these docs.`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="First Analysis"
  accent="Your first structural map."
  description="Run Flowprint 0.1.0-alpha.5 on a Next.js repository: single apps, monorepo discovery, choosing an app with --app, and reading the first report."
  lead="Run Flowprint from your install folder against a Next.js repository, choose an app if there are several, and read the report in the order it is printed."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Section id="run" title="Run it on a repository" n={1}>
    <p>
      Always run Flowprint from the <code>flowprint-test</code> folder you created in the
      <a href="/docs/quick-start">Quick Start</a>, and pass the path to the repository or app you want to analyze. Put the
      path in quotes if it contains spaces.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "/path/to/repo"`} label="macOS · Linux" />
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "C:\\Users\\you\\code\\repo"`} label="Windows" />
    <ul>
      <li>You do <strong>not</strong> need to install the analyzed repository's dependencies.</li>
      <li>Flowprint only reads files. It does not start the app and does not modify the repository.</li>
      <li>Analysis time depends on the size of the app.</li>
    </ul>
    <Notice title="Without a path, Flowprint analyzes the current folder">
      <p>
        <code>npx flowprint</code> on its own analyzes the current directory — which is <code>flowprint-test</code>, not a
        Next.js app. You will get the “none detected” notice described below. Pass the repository path explicitly.
      </p>
    </Notice>
  </Section>

  <Section id="what-happens" title="What Flowprint does first" n={2}>
    <p>Flowprint first looks for supported Next.js apps under the path you gave it. There are three outcomes:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Found</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>One supported app</td><td>It is analyzed directly and you get a report.</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Several supported apps</td>
            <td>
              Flowprint lists them and shows how to pick one with <code>--app</code>. No app is guessed.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No supported app</td>
            <td>
              An honest notice and exit code <code>0</code>. This means none of Flowprint's checks matched — not that the
              repository contains no application.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Running at the root of a monorepo is fine — that is how discovery works. You can also pass an app's own folder
      directly. Here is discovery on a two-app pnpm workspace:
    </p>
    <Terminal output={discovery} command="npx flowprint ~/code/harbor-workspace" title="Discovery — two supported apps">
      {caption} Absolute paths shortened to <code>~/code</code>.
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="choose-app" title="Choosing an app with --app" n={3}>
    <p>
      <code>--app</code> selects one Next.js application in a workspace. It does not filter by router: an app using App
      Router, Pages Router, or both is analyzed as a whole. The selector can be:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>a <strong>package name</strong> from the app's <code>package.json</code>, e.g. <code>@harbor/web</code>;</li>
      <li>a <strong>repo-relative path</strong>, e.g. <code>apps/web</code>;</li>
      <li><code>.</code> for the app at the repository root.</li>
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "/path/to/repo" --app apps/web`} label="Select by path" />
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "/path/to/repo" --app @harbor/web`} label="Select by package name" />
    <p>
      If the selector matches nothing, or matches more than one package, Flowprint prints the available options and exits
      with code <code>2</code>. Details are in the <a href="/docs/cli-reference#app">CLI Reference</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="first-report" title="Your first report" n={4}>
    <p>
      This is the default report for <code>field-notes</code>, a single Next.js app that uses both App Router and Pages
      Router, a dynamic segment, a server action module, and a middleware rewrite:
    </p>
    <Terminal output={report} command="npx flowprint ~/code/field-notes" title="Default report">
      {caption}
    </Terminal>
    <p>The report always has the same shape: a header, then five sections.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Section</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Header</td><td>Version, analyzed app, time taken, and the detected framework with its reason.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Start here</td><td>Entry points worth opening first, each with why it matters.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Routes at a glance</td><td>Counts of route files by kind and router, dynamic segments, top-level paths.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Runtime blind spots</td><td>What cannot be settled statically, grouped by kind, each <Tag t="U" /> with a reason.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Analysis statistics, what was skipped or unreadable, and the detection scope.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Next</td><td>A suggested follow-up, such as re-running with <code>--full</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="reading-order" title="A reading order" n={5}>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Start here.</strong> Open these files first. In the example: the root layout, the root page, and
        <code>middleware.ts</code>, which runs before routing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Routes at a glance.</strong> Get the shape: five route files, mostly App Router, one Pages Router API
        route, one dynamic segment (<code>[noteId]</code>). A dynamic segment in a file path is a structural fact.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Runtime blind spots.</strong> This is your list of things to verify by other means. Here: which concrete
        note URLs exist, and what the middleware rewrite actually does at runtime.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Coverage.</strong> Check what was not analyzed before trusting a summary. An empty list of findings is
        not a guarantee.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Next.</strong> Follow the hint — usually <code>--full</code> for the complete report.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      For the precise meaning of each label and common misreadings, see
      <a href="/docs/reading-output">Reading the Output</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="full" title="Going further with --full" n={6}>
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "/path/to/repo" --full`} label="Any shell" />
    <p>
      <code>--full</code> prints the complete report: every modeled route, finding, and procedure, with no truncation. In
      addition to the default sections it lists all routes with their source files, server actions, aliases, tRPC
      procedures, import probes, and the exact list of frameworks the detection checked. It does not add new kinds of
      analysis — it shows everything that was modeled. <code>--full</code> can be combined with <code>--app</code>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="saving" title="Keeping a copy" n={7}>
    <p>
      Flowprint prints to standard output. To keep a report, use your shell's ordinary output redirection — this is a
      shell feature, not a Flowprint option:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={`npx flowprint "/path/to/repo" > flowprint-report.txt`} label="Shell redirection" />
    <p>
      Flowprint has no built-in snapshot, diff, or JSON output in alpha.5.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
